1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
PatchCourt catches silent compatibility regressions at the framework-to-provider boundary.
This is a real local OFFLINE recording. No live model inference is shown.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The first case executes a pinned upstream NVIDIA adapter function locally.
The candidate loses tool calls while their results remain: a broken protocol.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The findings identify orphan tool results and missing calls.
This is a protocol verdict backed by recorded execution, not an LLM opinion.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:42,000
A deterministic local repair preserves the structured tool calls.
The replay passes after repair; this patch was not generated by a model.

5
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Before repair, the inventory task cannot associate requests with results.
After repair, the deterministic replay restores the inventory value to 21.

6
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The CrewAI source case exposes an image reference lost during formatting.
A successful API response alone would not prove that image semantics survived.

7
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The provenance records upstream source, hashes, and the execution boundary.
Source-function evidence is narrower than full SDK or production verification.

8
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Verified observations are saved with version, source, and environment scope.
Knowledge reuse never skips executing the current compatibility contract.

9
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Nebius/Nemotron and Tavily credentials are unavailable. The live lab is BLOCKED.
This demo claims no live inference, model benchmark, or retrieval improvement.

10
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Acceptance gates expose what passed and what still needs evidence.
Local repairs and controlled mutations do not establish universal compatibility.

11
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Source: github.com/karurukaruru/agent-code-validator
Run: python -m patchcourt demo --output outputs/demo
